sdata> -- Issue #70 / ADR-055: this is the test that actually discriminates a
sdata> -- correct implementation from a naive one. A LET typed at the REPL prompt
sdata> -- is queued into Active_Program_Vec (invisible to a purely Step_Start-based
sdata> -- batch-style flush, since each REPL immediate command reaches
sdata> -- SData.Interpreter.Execute as a fresh singleton-statement call). The
sdata> -- implicit-RUN trigger for SORT must detect this via
sdata> -- Active_Program_Vec.Is_Empty and call Run_Active_Program -- not silently
sdata> -- skip the LET. If it did skip it, Z would never be created and SORT Z
sdata> -- would raise "undefined variable" instead of succeeding with 4 variables.
sdata> USE "tests/data/freq_select.csv"
Dataset opened: tests/data/freq_select.csv
sdata> LET Z = ID * -1
sdata> SORT Z
RUN complete. 6 records and 4 variables processed.
SORT complete. 6 records and 4 variables processed.
sdata> QUIT
